 List and Pettit's framework implies a non‐contingent relationship between good corporate agents and egalitarian structures. This article makes two contributions: (1) it enriches debates in the political theory of the firm by clarifying how group agency concepts impact the normative understanding of corporate structures; (2) it strengthens the normative foundations of List and Pettit's framework by situating their theoretical criteria within empirical evidence of organizational effectiveness."}]", "excerpt_truncated": true, "source_sha256": "687783f692a88b9fb434081c3ab143776d2570b71f9ac495793c9b0124571f6b", "verification_required": true, "topic_domain": "obedience_limits", "evidence_role": "discovery", "host_tier": "discovery", "source_identity": null, "persistent_identifiers": ["doi:10.1016/j.jecp.2011.04.003", "doi:10.1002/japp.70071", "openalex:W2001333581", "openalex:W3140759188", "openalex:W7125497011"], "source_candidates": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jecp.2011.04.003", "https://doi.org/10.1002/japp.70071", "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/pdfdirect/10.1002/japp.70071"], "source_candidate_identities": {}}
